2\\bigl(a-i\\bigr)+i\n  =\\frac12\\bigl(a-(a-b+1)\\bigr)+(a-b+1)\n  =\\frac12(b-1)+(a-b+1)=b .\n\\]\n\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).  Since the choice of \\((a,b)\\) was arbitrary, **every point of \\(\\mathcal P_t\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{t-1}\\)**. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\); all of them are non‑sunny, so the required configuration exists.\n\n* If \\(k\\ge 1\\) set \\(m:=n-k\\).  \n  – Use the \\(m\\) horizontal lines \\(H_j\\) defined in (6).  \n  – By Lemma 2.1 it remains to cover the set \\(\\mathcal P_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n  – Apply the construction of Section 3 with \\(t=k\\); this provides \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) that cover \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).  \n  – Translate each of these \\(k\\) lines upward by \\(m\\) units (as described in the proof of Lemma 2.1) so that they now cover the points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose ordinate exceeds \\(m\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Because the trivial bound (5) shows that no larger value of \\(k\\) is possible, the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,1,2,\\dots ,n}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
