olution**\n\nLet \\(n=3\\).  \nThe requirement \u201cfor all positive integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the lines\u2019\u2019 forces the six lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nto be covered.\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\\(x\\)-axis \\((y=0)\\), \\(y\\)-axis \\((x=0)\\), or \\(x+y=0\\).  \nEquivalently, a sunny line must have a slope different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The unavoidable non\u2011sunny line\n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,3),\\qquad B=(2,2),\\qquad C=(3,1)\n\\]\n\nsatisfy \\(x+y=4\\); hence they are collinear on  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{0}:\\;x+y=4.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\), it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\nSuppose a configuration uses **no** line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThen each of \\(A,B,C\\) must lie on a distinct line, because any line through two of them is precisely \\(\\ell_{0}\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \nWith only three lines total and the requirement that exactly two of them be sunny, we would have at most one non\u2011sunny line, which is insufficient to cover the three points \\(A,B,C\\).  \nHence any admissible configuration must contain \\(\\ell_{0}\\) as the **unique** non\u2011sunny line.\n\nThus we fix  \n\n\\[\nL_{3}= \\ell_{0}: x+y=4,\n\\]\n\nwhich already covers \\(A,B,C\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Points still to be covered\n\nAfter accounting for \\(L_{3}\\), the uncovered points are  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe remaining two lines, \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\), must be sunny and together cover all points of \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Any line through two points of \\(R\\) is non\u2011sunny\n\nCompute the slopes of the three possible pairs of points in \\(R\\):\n\n* \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\): vertical line \\(x=1\\) (slope \\(\\infty\\));\n* \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\): horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (slope \\(0\\));\n* \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\): slope \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1-2}{2-1}=-1\\).\n\nAll three slopes are prohibited for a sunny line. Consequently **no sunny line can contain two distinct points of \\(R\\)**; a sunny line can intersect \\(R\\) in at most one point.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Impossibility with only two sunny lines\n\nSince each sunny line can cover at most one point of \\(R\\), the two sunny lines \\(L_{1},L_{2}\\) can cover at most two points of \\(R\\).  \nThe third point of \\(R\\) would remain uncovered, contradicting the covering condition (1).\n\nTherefore a configuration of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines that satisfies the given covering requirement does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there is **no** arrangement of three distinct lines in the plane that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1,\\ a+b\\le4\\) and has exactly \\(k=2\\) sunny lines. Hence the requested construction is impossible."
