01c*If \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd)*, then \\(m=t+1\\) and (2) becomes\n> \\[\n> \\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2\\le k(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1).\n> \\]\n> Simplifying yields \\(k\\in\\{0,2t+1\\}\\); i.e. only \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\) are possible.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nCarrying out the algebra:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(2t+1)(2t+2)}2 = (2t+1)(t+1).\n\\]\n\nThe right\u2011hand side simplifies to\n\n\\[\nk(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1) = (2t+1)^2 - k t.\n\\]\n\nThus the inequality is\n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1) \\le (2t+1)^2 - k t.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\((2t+1)(t+1)\\) from both sides gives\n\n\\[\n0 \\le (2t+1)^2 - (2t+1)(t+1) - k t = t[(2t+1)-k].\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t>0\\), this is equivalent to \\(k \\le 2t+1 = n\\), which is **trivial** (every configuration has at most \\(n\\) sunny lines). The claim that the inequality forces \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\) is **incorrect**. \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Substitution for even \\(n\\)  \n\n> \u201c*If \\(n=2t\\) (even)*, then \\(m=t\\) and (2) reads\n> \\[\n> \\frac{2t(2t+1)}2\\le k t+(2t-k)2t,\n> \\]\n> which simplifies to the trivial inequality \\(0\\le k\\le 2t\\).  \n> Hence **no restriction** on \\(k\\) arises from (2) when \\(n\\) is even.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe algebra is correct; the inequality reduces to \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), which imposes no extra restriction beyond the obvious one. This step is sound.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Construction for \\(k=0\\) (all non\u2011sunny)  \n\n> \u201cTake the \\(n\\) diagonals\n> \\[\n> L_i:\\;x+y=i+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n> \\tag{3}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nEach line \\(x+y=i+1\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (hence non\u2011sunny). For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), so the point lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_{a+b-1}\\). The lines are distinct and there are \\(n\\) of them. Hence the construction satisfies the requirements for \\(k=0\\). This step is correct.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all sunny)  \n\n> \u201cFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) define the line\n> \\[\n> S_i:\\;y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x .\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]  \n> \u2026 One checks that \\(S_i\\) contains the two points \\((i,i)\\) and \\((i+1,i-1)\\) (both belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)), and that the collection \\(\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Substituting \\((i,i)\\) into \\(y=\\frac{i}{i+1}x\\) gives \\(i = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,i = \\frac{i^{2}}{i+1}\\), which holds only for \\(i=0\\). For any \\(i\\ge1\\) the equality fails, so \\((i,i)\\) is **not** on \\(S_i\\).  \n- Similarly, \\((i+1,i-1)\\) does not satisfy the equation.  \n- Consequently the claimed covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) by the lines \\(S_i\\) is false; many points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) are not on any of these lines.  \nThus the construction for the case \\(k=n\\) is invalid. \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Mixed case for even \\(n\\) (\\(0<k<n\\))  \n\n> \u201cFor \\(j=1,\\dots ,k\\) set\n> \\[\n> S'_j:\\; y = \\frac{j}{j+2}\\,x + \\frac{2}{j+2}.\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> One checks that \\(S'_j\\) contains the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((j+2,j)\\), both belonging to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\), and that the sets of points covered by the different \\(S'_j\\) are pairwise disjoint.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- Plugging \\((1,1)\\) into the equation yields \\(1 = \\frac{j}{j+2}\\cdot1 + \\frac{2}{j+2} = \\frac{j+2}{j+2}=1\\); this holds, so \\((1,1)\\) is on every \\(S'_j\\).  \n- Plugging \\((j+2,j)\\) gives \\(j = \\frac{j}{j+2}(j+2) + \\frac{2}{j+2} = j + \\frac{2}{j+2}\\), which would require \\(\\frac{2}{j+2}=0\\), impossible for any finite \\(j\\). Hence \\((j+2,j)\\) is **not** on \\(S'_j\\).  \n- Because all \\(S'_j\\) share the point \\((1,1)\\), they are not pairwise disjoint in the set of covered points.  \nTherefore the claimed properties of the lines \\(S'_j\\) are false, and the mixed\u2011case construction collapses. \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Impossibility proof for odd \\(n\\) and \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)  \n\n> \u201cAssume \\(n=2t+1\\) is odd and that a configuration with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists. \u2026 Consequently\n> \\[\n> \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le k(t+1)+(n-k)(2t+1)= (2t+1)n -k t .\n> \\]  \n> Rearranging gives \\(k t\\le 0\\), which forces \\(k=0\\).\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nStarting from the displayed inequality and substituting \\(n=2t+1\\) yields\n\n\\[\n(2t+1)(t+1) \\le (2t+1)^2 - k t.\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\((2t+1)(t+1)\\) from both sides gives\n\n\\[\n0 \\le (2t+1)^2 - (2t+1)(t+1) - k t = t[(2t+1)-k].\n\\]\n\nSince \\(t>0\\), this inequality is equivalent to \\(k \\le 2t+1 = n\\), which is already known. The step \u201crearranging gives \\(k t\\le 0\\)\u201d is **incorrect**; the algebra does not produce that inequality. Consequently the argument that intermediate values of \\(k\\) are impossible for odd \\(n\\) is invalid. \u2192 **Critical Error**.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Overall conclusion  \n\n> \u201c\\[\n> \\boxed{\\text{For odd }n:\\;k\\in\\{0,n\\};\\qquad\n> \\text{For even }n:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \nBecause the impossibility proof for odd \\(n\\) is flawed and the construction for the case \\(k=n\\) is incorrect, the claimed classification is not established. Moreover, a simple counterexample for \\(n=3\\) (the three lines \\(y=x\\), \\(x+y=3\\), \\(x+y=4\\) give \\(k=1\\)) shows that the statement \u201conly \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is odd\u201d is false. Hence the final answer is incorrect.\n\n--- \n\n**Conclusion:** The solution contains multiple critical errors (incorrect algebraic deductions, false constructions, and an invalid impossibility proof) and a justification gap in the proof of Lemma\u202f1. Consequently the answer does not correctly solve the problem."

