to one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).\n\n   *Points with abscissa \\(\\ge k+1\\).*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  \n   By definition it lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (2).  \n   The lines \\(V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\) are pairwise distinct, so each such\n   point is covered exactly once.\n\n   Hence the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\n   sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n   \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   Because the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), and the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) holds for any\n   configuration, the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely\n   \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ and every }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\text{ there exist }n\\text{ distinct lines with exactly }k\\text{ sunny.}}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n:=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); equivalently the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Trivial upper bound\n\nOnly \\(n\\) lines are allowed, therefore the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\nsatisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove that **every** integer satisfying (2) can actually be\nrealised.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The sunny lines\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y = x + (n+1-2i) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nA direct substitution shows that \\(S_i\\) contains the points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad\nQ_i=(i-1,\\;n-i)\\;(i\\ge 2).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBoth points satisfy \\(x+y\\le n+1\\); hence they belong to \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nFor \\(i=1\\) the second point does not exist, and \\(S_1\\) contains only\n\\(P_1=(1,n)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The non\u2011sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\; x=j .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is a vertical line, therefore non\u2011sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The whole family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines; precisely the\nfirst \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Proof that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Points with first coordinate \\(\\le k\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\).\n\n*If \\(a=k\\):* the point is \\(P_k\\) and lies on \\(S_k\\).\n\n*If \\(a<k\\):* the point is \\(P_a\\) and lies on \\(S_a\\).\n\nThus every point whose abscissa does not exceed \\(k\\) belongs to a sunny\nline of the family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Points with first coordinate \\(\\ge k+1\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\ge k+1\\).  \nBy definition the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which is present in\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).  Hence all such points are covered\nby the non\u2011sunny part of the family.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  No point is missed\n\nThe two cases above exhaust all possibilities for the first coordinate,\nso every element of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of\n\\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the proof\n\nWe have exhibited, for an arbitrary integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\n\n* a set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n* exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny,\n* every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the set.\n\nTherefore each such \\(k\\) is attainable.  \nCombined with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) proved in (2), we obtain the\nfull description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
