ement of \\(A\\) and an element of \\(B\\) is\n\\(2\\) and the largest is \\(2n\\)).  Such a pair would violate (5) because\n\\(a+b\\ge n+2\\) contradicts the defining inequality of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\nHence **at least one** of the sets \\(A,B\\) must have cardinality at most \\(1\\).\n\nConsequences:\n\n* If \\(|A|\\le 1\\) then \\(n-|V|\\le 1\\), i.e. \\(|V|\\ge n-1\\).\n  Since \\(|V|+|H|=n-k\\), we obtain \\(n-1+|H|\\le n-k\\), whence\n  \\(|H|\\le k-1\\).  But \\(|H|\\ge 0\\), so \\(k\\ge 1\\).  Moreover,\n  \\(|V|\\ge n-1\\) forces \\(|H|\\le 1\\).  If \\(|H|=1\\) then the unique\n  horizontal line would have to contain all points whose ordinate is the\n  missing value in \\(B\\); this is impossible because those points have\n  different abscissae.  Therefore \\(|H|=0\\) and \\(|V|=n\\); all \\(n\\) lines are\n  vertical, i.e. **no line is sunny**.  Hence \\(k=0\\).\n\n* If \\(|B|\\le 1\\) the symmetric argument yields \\(|H|\\ge n-1\\) and forces\n  \\(|V|=0\\).  Thus all lines are horizontal, again giving \\(k=0\\).\n\nThe only remaining possibility is that **both** \\(|A|=|B|=1\\).  Then\n\\(|V|=|H|=n-1\\) and \\(|V|+|H|=2n-2=n-k\\), which gives \\(k=n\\).  In this case\nevery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) must lie on one of the two non\u2011sunny lines,\nwhich is impossible because the two lines are parallel to different axes.\nHence the case \\(|A|=|B|=1\\) cannot occur.\n\nTherefore the only values of \\(k\\) compatible with a covering by \\(n\\) lines are  \n\n\\[\nk=0\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad k=n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) has \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on the\n  line \\(x=a\\).  All these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none\n  of them is sunny.  Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y = x + (n+1-2i) .\n  \\tag{8}\n  \\]\n\n  The slope of each \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n  The points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).\n  Substituting \\(b\\) from (8) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  a + \\bigl(a + (n+1-2i)\\bigr) = 2a + n+1-2i \\le n+1\n  \\Longleftrightarrow a\\le i .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence for each \\(i\\) the line \\(L_i\\) contains exactly the points\n  \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le i\\) and \\(b = a + (n+1-2i)\\); in particular it\n  contains the two lattice points  \n\n  \\[\n  (i,\\;n+1-i),\\qquad (i-1,\\;n-i) \\;(i\\ge 2).\n  \\tag{9}\n  \\]\n\n  Varying \\(i\\) from \\(1\\) to \\(n\\) yields all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\):\n  every admissible pair \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n\\); choose \\(i=a\\); then\n  \\(b = n+1-a\\) or \\(b = n-a\\) and the corresponding point appears in (9).\n  Consequently the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) covers the whole set\n  \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  All \\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving a configuration with\n  \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe analysis shows that the only possible numbers of sunny lines in a family\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  Both values are attainable by the explicit constructions\nabove.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values are }k=0\\text{ and }k=n.}\n\\]"
